= Composition and Levels of Organization

Our previous inquiry into the model of application development abstracted away the environment. Our focus were small communities or groups with a common interest, that were local, situated. But if we split the world into small communities, what we get is a great number of communities that need to interact with each other. As we have mentioned, the specification is determined by the needs of the community and the state of the environment, thus by the needs and interests of the other communities.

When can we say that a group of communities complement each other? In some way, we must be able to express, that the needs of a community are fulfilled by the other communities. Thus here, we do not only want to have a model that describes the changes of a community as it interacts with the environment. We want to express that some interactions with the environment will always happen eventually. #note([Is there a way to describe that they will happen often? I think so...]).

What kind of information does the specification express?

It is important to understand that the interface of the community with the environment continually changes, because the internal state of the application changes as the members of the community interact with each other. What this means is that
- the way the system changes due to an interaction with the environment is constantly evolving,
- the structure of needs(inputs) and providers(outputs) of the community also changes. 


